\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Components of a split endomorphism}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{To eliminate part of a field, first choose which part is retained.
Let #{R} be a commutative unital \vocabk{ring}{ca-000U} and
#{M,X,Y} unital \vocabk{#{R}-modules}{ca-000X}. A
\newvocab{chosen binary splitting} of #{M} consists of ordinary
#{R}-linear maps
##{\iota_X:X\to M,\quad \iota_Y:Y\to M,\qquad
   \pi_X:M\to X,\quad \pi_Y:M\to Y}
satisfying all five identities
##{\begin{aligned}
\pi_X\iota_X&=I_X,&\pi_Y\iota_Y&=I_Y,\\
\pi_X\iota_Y&=0,&\pi_Y\iota_X&=0,\\
\iota_X\pi_X+\iota_Y\pi_Y&=I_M.
\end{aligned}}
Products apply the right factor first, as in
[composition and field multiplication](fdip-0011).
The maps #{(x,y)\mapsto\iota_Xx+\iota_Yy} and
#{z\mapsto(\pi_Xz,\pi_Yz)} are inverse linear maps by these identities.
We consequently write #{M=X\oplus Y} and #{z=(x,y)} relative to
this chosen identification.}

\tikzfigs{0.7}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  carrier/.style={draw,rounded corners,minimum width=10mm,
    minimum height=8mm,inner sep=2mm},
  map/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},thick}
]
\node[carrier] (x) at (0,0) {$X$};
\node[carrier] (m) at (3.2,0) {$M$};
\node[carrier] (y) at (6.4,0) {$Y$};
\draw[map] (x) to[bend left=20] node[above=2mm] {$\iota_X$} (m);
\draw[map] (m) to[bend left=20] node[below=2mm] {$\pi_X$} (x);
\draw[map] (m) to[bend left=20] node[above=2mm] {$\pi_Y$} (y);
\draw[map] (y) to[bend left=20] node[below=2mm] {$\iota_Y$} (m);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{Each summand is included in #{M} and recovered by its projection.
The composite #{\pi_X\iota_X} is #{I_X}, whereas
#{\iota_X\pi_X} is an endomorphism of #{M}. In the
[partition realization](fdip-001I), these are restriction and
extension by zero; the latter composite is the support projection.}

\p{For an endomorphism #{Q:M\to M}, its
\newvocab{components for the chosen splitting} are
##{\begin{aligned}
A&=\pi_XQ\iota_X:X\to X,&B&=\pi_XQ\iota_Y:Y\to X,\\
E&=\pi_YQ\iota_X:X\to Y,&C&=\pi_YQ\iota_Y:Y\to Y.
\end{aligned}}
Inserting #{I_M=\iota_X\pi_X+\iota_Y\pi_Y} gives
##{Q(x,y)=(Ax+By,Ex+Cy).}
If the eliminated component #{C} is an
\vocabk{isomorphism}{ca-0015}, define the
\newvocab{split Schur map}, relative to this splitting and pivot, by
##{S=A-BC^{-1}E:X\longrightarrow X.}
Changing the splitting or the pivot changes the construction.}

\p{The module convention is
\citet{Section 10.10, Tag 0581}{stacks2017stacks}.
Mathlib's \leanref{CategoryTheory.Biprod.ofComponents} gives
components in a preadditive category with binary biproducts.
Its arguments index source then target; after ordering summands
#{(Y,X)}, the arguments corresponding to #{Q} are #{(C,B,E,A)}.}

\p{\citet{Appendix A.5.5, pp. 650-651}{boyd2004convex} treats the real
\vocabk{Schur complement}{fdip-000D}.
The source eliminates its first block: exchanging its variables to
#{(y,x)} makes its block order #{(C,E,B,A)} and its residual
#{A-BC^{-1}E}. The chosen-splitting definition above uses only linear
maps; it does not assume a basis, pairing or positivity.}

\p{The [ring elimination example](fdip-001Z) computes the components
and Schur map for a concrete chosen splitting.}
